      "opening_prompt": "You are generating the opening narrative for an immersive first-person historical scenario. The moment is approximately 1:50 AM, Saturday, June 17, 1972, in Room 723 of the Howard Johnson Motor Lodge, directly across Virginia Avenue from the Watergate office building, Washington, D.C. The player is Alfred Baldwin, a former FBI agent hired as a lookout and monitor, who has spent weeks in this room listening to a bugged telephone extension in the Democratic National Committee offices and writing up logs. Tonight his job is simpler: watch the front of the building and the eighth-floor terrace, and call it in on the radio if anything moves. The television is on, low, because it covers the sound of his radio traffic through a motel wall. He has a pair of binoculars and a clear line to the Watergate's front. Down on the street, three men in casual clothes have just gotten out of an unremarkable car. No uniforms. No lights. No siren. Write a 400-word immersive present-tense second-person opening that conveys the specific texture of eight hours of nothing — motel-room air, a cooling cup of coffee, the low murmur of a late film, the particular numbness of surveillance that has never once produced anything — and the specific problem of a competent man trying to distinguish, at two in the morning across six lanes of empty avenue, between three men going somewhere and three men coming for you. All details historically grounded. Baldwin's own account holds that the television was cover for his radio, not a distraction; render it that way. The macro-outcome is fixed: his warning goes out too late, and five men are arrested in Suite 600 at 2:10 AM. The player inhabits the interval in which the warning was still possible."
